ZyperOS: on Mesa's Panfrost, framebuffer copies are blits, not
glCopyImageSubData, so they stay on the GPU.

PPSSPP copies a framebuffer with glCopyImageSubData when the driver has the
copy-image extension, and with glBlitFramebuffer when it has not
(FramebufferManagerCommon::BlitFramebuffer). Mesa's Panfrost (the Pixel 2's
Mali-G31) has the extension but no GPU path behind it: its
resource_copy_region is util_resource_copy_region, which maps both images,
waiting for the GPU to finish rendering the source (PANFROST_WAIT_BO), and
copies the tiled pixels on the CPU. A blit is a GPU job that waits for
nothing on the CPU.

God of War: Chains of Olympus's main menu textures from its own render target
six times a frame (PPSSPP's self-texture copies). On a GKD Pixel 2 those six
copies held the GL thread for 25 ms of every frame, most of it in
PANFROST_WAIT_BO.

With this patch, on the same screen: 20.1 to 31.3 frames a second (median
frame 49.9 to 33.3 ms), PANFROST_WAIT_BO in 61 of 600 samples of the GL
thread instead of 147. Daxter, which makes no such copies, 30.0 before and
after. The pictures match.

The blit is the same size and NEAREST, so the same pixels; it is the path
PPSSPP takes on every OpenGL ES 3 driver without the copy-image extension.
Only Mesa's Panfrost (GL_VENDOR "Mesa", GL_RENDERER "... (Panfrost)") is
changed.

Upstream: the same holds for any Panfrost device running PPSSPP's OpenGL
backend.

diff --git a/Common/GPU/OpenGL/thin3d_gl.cpp b/Common/GPU/OpenGL/thin3d_gl.cpp
index 046ab99..3b4b83e 100644
--- a/Common/GPU/OpenGL/thin3d_gl.cpp
+++ b/Common/GPU/OpenGL/thin3d_gl.cpp
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 #include <cstdio>
+#include <cstring>
 #include <vector>
 #include <string>
 #include <map>
@@ -585,6 +586,11 @@ OpenGLContext::OpenGLContext(bool canChangeSwapInterval) : renderManager_(frameT
 	caps_.dualSourceBlend = gl_extensions.ARB_blend_func_extended || gl_extensions.EXT_blend_func_extended;
 	caps_.anisoSupported = gl_extensions.EXT_texture_filter_anisotropic;
 	caps_.framebufferCopySupported = gl_extensions.OES_copy_image || gl_extensions.NV_copy_image || gl_extensions.EXT_copy_image || gl_extensions.ARB_copy_image;
+	// Mesa's Panfrost has no GPU path for glCopyImageSubData: it maps both images (waiting for the GPU
+	// to finish the source) and copies them on the CPU. A same-size blit is the same pixels, on the GPU.
+	if (gl_extensions.gpuVendor == GPU_VENDOR_MESA && strstr(gl_extensions.model, "(Panfrost)")) {
+		caps_.framebufferCopySupported = false;
+	}
 	caps_.framebufferBlitSupported = gl_extensions.NV_framebuffer_blit || gl_extensions.ARB_framebuffer_object || gl_extensions.GLES3;
 	caps_.framebufferDepthBlitSupported = caps_.framebufferBlitSupported;
 	caps_.framebufferStencilBlitSupported = caps_.framebufferBlitSupported;
